buildERing -- creates a ring to be used with other functions in the EquivariantGB package
*****************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        R = buildERing(X,I,F,n)
  * Inputs:
      * X, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of symbols, one for each
        block of variables
      * I, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of integers, the number of
        indices for each variable block
      * F, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, the coefficient ring
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the width bound
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Degrees => ..., default value {}
      * MonomialOrder => ..., default value Lex
  * Outputs:
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, a polynomial ring with
        coefficient field F, and a block of variables for each entry of X. The
        corresponding integers in I determine how many indices each block of
        variables has. Variables with all index values from 0 to n-1 are
        included.

Description
===========

The object produced by buildERing is a *note Ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring, with
additional stored information. It is used to represent a polynomial ring F[Y]
with an infinite set of variables Y, and an action of the infinite symmetric
group on Y such that it is composed of a finite number of orbits.  Macaulay2
cannot store a ring with an infinite number of variables, so the ring produced
contains only some of the variables.

We assume Y decomposes into a finite number of "blocks",  each block consisting
of variables of the form x_{(i_1,...,i_k)} for some k and each index i_j
ranging over all non-negative integers.  The symbol x to be used for each block
is listed in X.  The value of k for each block is listed in I.  The variables
in the ring output by buildERing are those with index values ranging from 0 to
n-1.  The same ring with a different index bound n can be produced by *note
buildERing(Ring,ZZ): buildERing_lpRing_cmZZ_rp,.

+-------------------------------------------+
|i1 : S = buildERing({symbol z}, {1}, QQ, 4)|
|                                           |
|o1 = S                                     |
|                                           |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                        |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i2 : vars S                                |
|                                           |
|o2 = | z_3 z_2 z_1 z_0 |                   |
|                                           |
|             1      4                      |
|o2 : Matrix S  <-- S                       |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i3 : coefficientRing S                     |
|                                           |
|o3 = QQ                                    |
|                                           |
|o3 : Ring                                  |
+-------------------------------------------+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : R = buildERing({symbol y, symbol x}, {2,1}, QQ, 3)                      |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = R                                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : PolynomialRing                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : vars R                                                                  |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = | y_(2,2) y_(2,1) y_(2,0) y_(1,2) y_(1,1) y_(1,0) y_(0,2) y_(0,1)       |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     y_(0,0) x_2 x_1 x_0 |                                                   |
|                                                                             |
|             1      12                                                       |
|o5 : Matrix R  <-- R                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The monomial order can be chosen with optional argument *note MonomialOrder:
(Macaulay2Doc)MonomialOrder,. Currently valid choices are *note Lex:
(Macaulay2Doc)Lex,, *note GLex: (Macaulay2Doc)GLex, or *note GRevLex:
(Macaulay2Doc)GRevLex,. The variables are always ordered from the last block to
the first, with larger indices before smaller indices.  For blocks with
multiple indices the order of the variables is also lexicographic with first
index most significant, followed by the second, etc.

The grading of the ring can be chosen with optional argument *note Degrees:
(Macaulay2Doc)Degrees,. The grading is specified by listing the degree of each
variable block. Because the degree function must be invariant under the
symmetric action, every variable in the same block must have the same degree.

Ways to use buildERing:
=======================

  * "buildERing(List,List,Ring,ZZ)"
  * *note buildERing(Ring,ZZ): buildERing_lpRing_cmZZ_rp, -- creates a ERing
    from existing ERing

For the programmer
==================

The object *note buildERing: buildERing, is a *note method function with
options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

