File: EquivariantGB.info, Node: PriorityQueue, Next: reduce, Prev: PrincipalSyzygies, Up: Top

PriorityQueue -- an efficient mutable priority queue implementation
*******************************************************************

Description
===========

A priority queue is a data structure for storing a collection of totally
ordered objects and keeping track of the minimum. This binomial heap
implementation allows for efficiently adding a new element to the queue,
accessing or deleting the minimum element, or merging two queues.  Efficiently
means time logarithmic in the size of the queue, or better.

+--------------------------------+
|i1 : Q = priorityQueue {3,7,1,5}|
|                                |
|o1 = PriorityQueue{...4...}     |
|                                |
|o1 : PriorityQueue              |
+--------------------------------+
|i2 : min Q                      |
|                                |
|o2 = 1                          |
+--------------------------------+
|i3 : deleteMin Q;               |
+--------------------------------+
|i4 : insert(Q,2);               |
+--------------------------------+
|i5 : min Q                      |
|                                |
|o5 = 2                          |
+--------------------------------+
|i6 : R = priorityQueue {4,6,8}; |
+--------------------------------+
|i7 : QR = mergePQ(Q,R);         |
+--------------------------------+
|i8 : length QR                  |
|                                |
|o8 = 7                          |
+--------------------------------+
* Menu:

* priorityQueue::               create a new PriorityQueue
* insert(PriorityQueue,Thing): insert_lpPriorityQueue_cmThing_rp.  insert a new element into the queue
* min(PriorityQueue): min_lpPriorityQueue_rp.  return the minimum element of the queue
* deleteMin::                   deletes the minimum element of the queue
* pop::                         returns the minimum element of the queue and deletes it
* mergePQ::                     merges two queues
* length(PriorityQueue): length_lpPriorityQueue_rp.  returns the number of elements in the queue

Methods that use an object of class PriorityQueue:
==================================================

  * "deleteMin(PriorityQueue)" -- see *note deleteMin: deleteMin, -- deletes
    the minimum element of the queue
  * *note insert(PriorityQueue,Thing): insert_lpPriorityQueue_cmThing_rp, --
    insert a new element into the queue
  * *note length(PriorityQueue): length_lpPriorityQueue_rp, -- returns the
    number of elements in the queue
  * "mergePQ(PriorityQueue,PriorityQueue)" -- see *note mergePQ: mergePQ, --
    merges two queues
  * *note min(PriorityQueue): min_lpPriorityQueue_rp, -- return the minimum
    element of the queue
  * "pop(PriorityQueue)" -- see *note pop: pop, -- returns the minimum element
    of the queue and deletes it

For the programmer
==================

The object *note PriorityQueue: PriorityQueue, is a *note type:
(Macaulay2Doc)Type,, with ancestor classes *note MutableHashTable:
(Macaulay2Doc)MutableHashTable, < *note HashTable: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable, <
*note Thing: (Macaulay2Doc)Thing,.

